  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the points where the curve $x={t}^{2},\\,y=4t$ intersects the line $x+y+3=0.$\n\nThe intersection points are $(1,$\n{{field-1}}$)$ and $(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4t+3 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y:$ \n\n- We start with $t=-3.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4t \\\\  & =4(-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(9,-12).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-1.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4t \\\\  & =4(-1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(1,-4).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x={t}^{2},y=4t$ intersects the line $x+y+3=0$ at the points $(1,-4)$ and $(9,-12).$",
